# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
hobbes-leviathan-3.md:1: argue/readings: S9 is read 2 ways: hobbes-leviathan-3.logic.r2 has "fast_for_honour" and "fast_for_honour → ¬fast_to_obtain"; hobbes-leviathan-3.logic.r2.2 and hobbes-leviathan-3.logic.r2.3 have "fast_for_honour"; they differ when only "David's fasting for Saul, Jonathan and Abner was to obtain something for them at God's hands after their death" and "David's fasting for Saul and Abner was used for honour's sake to their persons" are true, which the second allows
hobbes-leviathan-3.md:1: argue/answered: S9: answered no, which keeps hobbes-leviathan-3.logic.r2, to: Suppose "David's fasting for Saul, Jonathan and Abner was to obtain something for them at God's hands after their death" and "David's fasting for Saul and Abner was used for honour's sake to their persons" are true. Is S9, "And thus with hard straining, hee has wrested those places to the proofe of a Purgatory; whereas it is manifest, that the ceremonies of Mourning, and Fasting, when they are used for the death of men, whose life was not profitable to the Mourners, they are used for honours sake to their persons; and when tis done for the death of them by whose life the Mourners had benefit, it proceeds from their particular dammage: And so David honoured Saul, and Abner, with his Fasting; and in the death of his owne child, recomforted himselfe, by receiving his ordinary food.", true then?
hobbes-leviathan-3.logic.r2: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: hobbes-leviathan-3.logic.r2 has "body_life_only → ¬natural_eternity" and "texts_prove → fast_to_obtain"; hobbes-leviathan-3.logic.r2.2 and hobbes-leviathan-3.logic.r2.3 have "fast_for_honour → ¬fast_to_obtain" and "texts_prove → fast_to_obtain"; they differ when only "separated souls have a natural eternity", "David's fasting for Saul, Jonathan and Abner was to obtain something for them at God's hands after their death" and "David's fasting for Saul and Abner was used for honour's sake to their persons" are true, which the first allows
hobbes-leviathan-3.logic.r2: argue/readings: U1: hobbes-leviathan-3.logic.r2 takes for granted "if there is no life but the life of the body, and no immortality till the Resurrection, then it is not so that separated souls have a natural eternity"; hobbes-leviathan-3.logic.r2.2 and hobbes-leviathan-3.logic.r2.3 do not
hobbes-leviathan-3.logic.r2: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps hobbes-leviathan-3.logic.r2, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if there is no life but the life of the body, and no immortality till the Resurrection, then it is not so that separated souls have a natural eternity"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
hobbes-leviathan-3.md:1: argue/converse: hobbes-leviathan-3.logic.r2 P2 (S2): the sentence makes the words after "no" the condition, but P2 has them follow from it: it reads "if eternal life is by grace only, then there is no life but the life of the body, and no immortality till the Resurrection"
hobbes-leviathan-3.md:1: argue/converse: hobbes-leviathan-3.logic.r2.2 P2 (S2): the sentence makes the words after "no" the condition, but P2 has them follow from it: it reads "if eternal life is by grace only, then there is no life but the life of the body, and no immortality till the Resurrection"
hobbes-leviathan-3.md:1: argue/converse: hobbes-leviathan-3.logic.r2.3 P2 (S2): the sentence makes the words after "no" the condition, but P2 has them follow from it: it reads "if eternal life is by grace only, then there is no life but the life of the body, and no immortality till the Resurrection"
